  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with the island, water, day/night sky and orbiting camera. It passes typecheck, but I could not run or build it here, so none of it has been seen rendering.\n\n- **Island:** the terrain is a flat-shaded mesh with one normal and one colour per face, split into sand, grass, rock and darker highland bands. It carries 22 trees and 14 rocks, all generated from code.\n- **Water:** a custom shader animates three summed waves and uses faceted normals to match the low-poly look. It adds fresnel, a sun glint, shallow-to-deep colour, and foam along the shoreline. It fades into the sky colour with distance so the plane's edge isn't visible.\n- **Day/night:** a full cycle takes 20 seconds, starting at hour 9. The Preetham sky (with stars) and the sun and moon lights move together each frame. Fog colour follows the time of day, a hemisphere fill light never drops to zero, and the moon lights the scene at night, so it is never black. The water shader's light direction, colour and brightness are updated each frame to match the sun or moon.\n- **Camera:** an orbit camera at about (0, 15, 27), looking at the island, with `autoRotate` 0.12 rad/s.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__ = true` is set after `app.ready()`.\n\nI'm least sure of two things. The water shader reads uniforms (`sunDirW`, `skyColW` and so on) in the fragment stage, and I assumed they are declared in both stages as the recipe suggests. The hemisphere light's `setLight` call is also unchecked.\n\n",
